  "narrative": "I assessed all newly delivered discovery results against the registered directions and the approximate 10% relative clinical scale. I used absolute outcome-magnitude benchmarks: SMARCA2 about 0.042, WRN about 0.030, USP7/NFE2L2 about 0.009, MDM2 about 0.027, and most other outcomes roughly 0.024-0.026. For direction=-1 outcomes, positive signed estimates mean stronger dependency; for direction=+1 outcomes, positive signed estimates mean weaker dependency.\n\nThe non-Avana library stress tests strengthen, rather than weaken, the accepted large dataset associations. H38 tests SMARCA4 loss versus SMARCA2 dependency within non-Avana screens. R27 is signed +0.8009, CI +0.7485 to +0.8534, n=61 versus 174. Although the exposed cell is small, the effect is enormous and precisely bounded above the SMARCA2 threshold. I accept H38 as a dataset-specific context association and keep it active because batch_b and broader technical context remain relevant. H41 tests TP53 loss versus USP7 dependency in non-Avana screens. R28 is signed +0.7674, CI +0.7031 to +0.8318, n=43 versus 192. This is far above the USP7 threshold of about 0.009 and supports the previously validated weaker-USP7-dependency association in the minority library stratum. I accept H41 as active. H46 tests KEAP1 loss versus NFE2L2 dependency in non-Avana screens. R29 is signed +0.7939, CI +0.7457 to +0.8422, n=40 versus 195, again far above the NFE2L2 threshold and supporting the reversed, validated direction of H14. I accept H46 as active. I do not interpret these as proving library-independent biology; rather, they reduce concern that the large effects are artifacts confined to the dominant Avana library.\n\nThe Avana-versus-non-Avana interaction tests are less decisive. H48 is a direction=-1 interaction for SMARCA4 loss on SMARCA2: positive signed interaction would mean the stronger-dependency association is more negative-raw in Avana relative to non-Avana. R30 is signed -0.0198, CI -0.0762 to +0.0365. The interval excludes clinically meaningful positive modification, because its upper bound is below the approximate SMARCA2 scale, but it does not exclude a clinically meaningful negative modification on the opposite side. Since the original registration was exploratory and the main effects are robust in both strata, I mark H48 unresolved and deferred rather than claim meaningful library modification. H49 is a direction=+1 interaction for KEAP1 loss on NFE2L2: positive signed interaction would mean the weaker-dependency association is stronger in Avana than in non-Avana. R31 is signed +0.0022, CI -0.0506 to +0.0550. Despite point near zero, the interval is wide and includes clinically meaningful positive and negative modification relative to the small NFE2L2 scale. I leave it unresolved and deferred. H50 is a direction=+1 interaction for TP53 loss on USP7: positive signed interaction would mean the weaker-USP7-dependency association is stronger in Avana than in non-Avana. R32 is signed +0.0383, CI -0.0292 to +0.1057. The point estimate exceeds the USP7 threshold and is scientifically interesting, but the interval still includes null and a clinically meaningful opposite modification. I leave H50 unresolved active because the combination of a large main effect and a non-trivial point interaction makes library modification potentially important, but the current evidence is not enough to accept it.\n\nThe co-mutation independence tests all support the specificity of the large associations. H51 tests TP53 loss versus weaker USP7 dependency among SMARCA4-wildtype samples. R33 is signed +0.8007, CI +0.7781 to +0.8233, n=317 versus 1271. This is enormous relative to the USP7 threshold and supports accepting that the TP53-USP7 association is not carried by SMARCA4 co-loss. I accept H51 as active. H52 tests SMARCA4 loss versus stronger SMARCA2 dependency among TP53-wildtype samples. R34 is signed +0.7873, CI +0.7657 to +0.8089. This supports accepting the SMARCA4-SMARCA2 association independently of TP53 co-loss. I accept H52 as active. H53 tests MSI-high versus stronger WRN dependency among TP53-wildtype samples. R35 is signed +0.7801, CI +0.7471 to +0.8130, far above the WRN threshold. I accept H53 as active. H54 tests KEAP1 loss versus weaker NFE2L2 dependency among TP53-wildtype samples. R36 is signed +0.7923, CI +0.7699 to +0.8147, far above the NFE2L2 threshold. I accept H54 as active. These tests do not eliminate all possible confounding by additional co-alterations or latent data structure, but they make simple co-mutation confounding by TP53 or SMARCA4 less plausible for the tested associations.\n\nThe null-control test is reassuring. H59 asks whether research_marker_d_positive, a marker stated to have no assigned gene or pathway, is associated with stronger SMARCA2 dependency. R37 is signed +0.00235, CI -0.0298 to +0.0345. The interval lies within the approximate ±0.042 SMARCA2 clinical band and is near zero. I reject the registered clinically meaningful marker-D association and close the investigation. Because this was a specificity control rather than a biological claim, this rejection is favorable: it reduces concern that large SMARCA2 effects are generic artifacts of constructed latent markers. It does not prove that the accepted associations are biologically causal or therapeutically generalizable.\n\nH55 tests MYC amplification versus stronger MDM2 dependency within TP53-wildtype samples. R38 is signed +0.00843, CI -0.0228 to +0.0397. The point estimate is below the approximate MDM2 threshold of 0.027, and the interval includes the null, although the upper bound does allow a clinically meaningful positive effect. I leave H55 unresolved and deferred. It is not accepted, but the current evidence is not sufficient to rule out a modest TP53-wildtype MYC-MDM2 association.\n\nI did not request validation this iteration. The newly accepted non-Avana and co-mutation results are already anchored by previously validated main effects or by multiple robust stratum/co-mutation tests, and the most valuable future validation may be reserved for borderline driver associations such as EGFR/KRAS contexts or, if needed later, for a focused library-interaction question. These remain dataset-specific associations, not causal, therapeutic, or generalizable claims.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 3 appraise update.\n\nCurrent accepted dataset-specific associations, all association-only: \n1. SMARCA4 loss -> stronger SMARCA2 dependency. Evidence now includes full population H7/R7, ARID1A-intact H15/R15, batch_a H25/R16, non-Avana H38/R27, and TP53-intact H52/R34. Very large and robust. Keep active pending batch_b and any remaining SWI/SNF/library-specific tests.\n2. MSI-high -> stronger WRN dependency. Evidence includes full H9/R9, Avana H26/R17, BRCA2-intact H18/R18, and TP53-intact H53/R35. Keep active pending batch_b and additional DNA-repair co-mutation checks.\n3. KEAP1 loss -> weaker NFE2L2 dependency, opposite canonical registered H8 direction. Main H14 validated; now non-Avana H46/R29 and TP53-intact H54/R36. Keep active pending STK11/KEAP1 interaction and any further co-alteration checks.\n4. TP53 loss -> weaker USP7 dependency. Main H20 validated; now non-Avana H41/R28 and SMARCA4-intact H51/R33. Keep active pending batch, KRAS context, Avana-only, and library-interaction interpretation.\n\nNegative or null-control conclusions from current and prior rounds include: KRAS mutation does not support stronger STAT3 or weaker RIT1 dependency; BRAF V600E does not support stronger BRAF dependency in KRAS-wildtype; KEAP1 loss does not support stronger NFE2L2 dependency in the originally registered direction; MYC amplification does not support stronger DHX9 or RPL11 dependency; ARID1A loss does not support stronger SMARCA2 dependency overall; ERBB2 amplification does not support stronger ERBB2 dependency in KRAS-wildtype; research_marker_d_positive does not support a clinically meaningful SMARCA2 association.\n\nLibrary interaction tests are not decisive. H48 SMARCA2/SMARCA4 Avana interaction is small and excludes clinically meaningful positive modification but not opposite modification; unresolved deferred. H49 NFE2L2/KEAP1 Avana interaction is inconclusive; unresolved deferred. H50 USP7/TP53 Avana interaction is point-positive beyond the USP7 threshold but CI includes null and opposite modification; unresolved active.\n\nRemaining unresolved/deferred scientific areas: borderline KRAS mutation -> stronger KRAS dependency; EGFR amplification contexts; overall ERBB2 amplification; TP53 loss -> weaker MDM2 dependency; BRCA2 loss -> WRN dependency; Avana/Ky/Humagne POLR2A technical benchmarks; MYC amplification -> stronger MDM2 dependency in TP53-wildtype H55, deferred; EGFR/TMED10 and PIK3CA/EGFR tests already registered but not yet run. Next analyses should prioritize batch_b and library/context checks for accepted signals, focused RTK/KRAS context refinements, and possibly technical benchmarks. Interpretation guardrail: large constructed-assay associations may reflect synthetic-lethal-like structure or latent data construction; do not infer causality or clinical generalizability without replication."
}

