  "narrative": "This synthesis incorporates the response deadline for H8 and the new independent validation for H15. I continue to use the 10% relative clinical benchmark by approximating an absolute scale from the absolute mean magnitude of each dependency score: SMARCA2 about 0.042, WRN about 0.030, NFE2L2 and USP7 about 0.009, MDM2 about 0.027, and most other outcomes about 0.024 to 0.028. For direction -1 claims, a positive signed estimate means stronger dependency; for direction +1 claims, a positive signed estimate means weaker dependency. Validation intervals are independent intervals that account for the maximum number of validation comparisons in the run.\n\nThe H8 response deadline is now resolved. H8 registered KEAP1 loss as associated with stronger NFE2L2 dependency. Discovery R8 was signed -0.7961, and validation R13 is signed -0.8096 with independent interval -0.8392 to -0.7799 and cell counts 415 versus 1585. Because these are negative under the direction -1 hypothesis, the raw comparison is the opposite: KEAP1 loss is associated with a higher NFE2L2 dependency score, i.e. weaker dependency. The validation is extremely large relative to the approximately 0.009 NFE2L2 threshold and the independent interval still excludes null and the registered direction. I therefore keep H8 rejected and closed. The same evidence supports the reversed claim H14, which is accepted: KEAP1 loss is associated with weaker NFE2L2 dependency in this supplied dataset.\n\nThe new validation for H15 strongly supports the SMARCA4-loss/SMARCA2-dependency signal. H15 asks whether SMARCA4 loss is associated with stronger SMARCA2 dependency in ARID1A-intact samples. Discovery R15 was signed +0.7770, 95% interval +0.7558 to +0.7983. Validation R39 is signed +0.7846 with independent interval +0.7517 to +0.8174 and cell counts 332 versus 1240. This is roughly 18-fold larger than the approximate SMARCA2 clinical scale and is essentially unchanged by validation. I therefore accept H15 and keep it active as part of the broader SMARCA2 context program. Together with H7/R7 overall +0.7840, H25 batch_a +0.7470, H38 non-Avana +0.8009, and H52 TP53-wildtype +0.7873, this supports a robust dataset-specific association between SMARCA4 loss and stronger SMARCA2 dependency that is not explained solely by ARID1A co-loss, a single batch, the dominant Avana library, or TP53 co-loss. This remains an association within the supplied dataset, not proof of causal synthetic lethality or clinical generalizability.\n\nThe supported dataset findings are now four internally consistent, very large, association-only signals. First, SMARCA4 loss is associated with stronger SMARCA2 dependency, with main and context-robust effects far above threshold. Second, MSI-high is associated with stronger WRN dependency: H9 overall +0.7899, H26 Avana +0.7945, H18 BRCA2-intact +0.7944, and H53 TP53-intact +0.7801, all far above the approximately 0.030 WRN scale. Third, KEAP1 loss is associated with weaker NFE2L2 dependency, opposite the original H8 direction, with discovery and validation large and precise, plus non-Avana and TP53-intact support. Fourth, TP53 loss is associated with weaker USP7 dependency: H20 overall validated at +0.8041, non-Avana H41 +0.7674, and SMARCA4-intact H51 +0.8007, all far above the approximately 0.009 USP7 scale.\n\nThe negative and null-control conclusions remain important. Besides H8 original, the dataset rejects KRAS mutation-associated stronger STAT3 dependency H2, BRAF V600E-associated stronger BRAF dependency among KRAS-wildtype samples H5, MYC amplification-associated stronger DHX9 dependency H10, MYC amplification-associated stronger RPL11 dependency H11, ARID1A loss-associated stronger SMARCA2 dependency overall H13, ERBB2 amplification-associated stronger ERBB2 dependency among KRAS-wildtype samples H27, KRAS mutation-associated weaker RIT1 dependency H32, and research_marker_d_positive as a clinically meaningful SMARCA2 predictor H59. These are conclusions about tested comparisons in this dataset, not universal biological exclusions.\n\nSeveral unresolved questions remain. H1 KRAS mutation versus stronger KRAS dependency is directionally supportive but small and imprecise: +0.0137, interval -0.0038 to +0.0312. H3 EGFR amplification is borderline: +0.0252, interval -0.0003 to +0.0507; H24 adenocarcinoma EGFR +0.0198, interval -0.0098 to +0.0494; H28 KRAS-wildtype EGFR +0.0162, interval -0.0135 to +0.0459. H33 TP53 loss versus stronger KIF18A dependency narrowly excludes zero, +0.0208, interval +0.0012 to +0.0403, but the point estimate is below the approximate KIF18A threshold. Overall ERBB2 amplification H4, TP53-loss versus weaker MDM2 dependency H6, Avana-library POLR2A benchmark H12, BRCA2 loss versus WRN dependency H17, and MYC amplification versus MDM2 dependency in TP53-wildtype cells H55 remain unresolved or deferred. Library interactions are also not decisive: H48 SMARCA2/SMARCA4 Avana interaction is small and excludes clinically meaningful positive modification but not opposite modification; H49 KEAP1/NFE2L2 Avana interaction is inconclusive; H50 USP7/TP53 Avana interaction has a positive point estimate above the USP7 threshold but a wide interval that includes null and opposite modification.\n\nThe most informative next analyses are to complete the already registered context and technical checks for the accepted signals, then pursue the strongest driver refinements. Priority should be SMARCA2 context tests H16 Avana-only, H39 batch_b, H21 ARID1A-interaction, and H31 ARID1A-loss in SMARCA4-intact samples; USP7 context tests H35 Avana-only, H36 batch_a, H42 batch_b, and H37 KRAS-wildtype; KEAP1/STK11 interaction H22; WRN batch_b H40; KIF18A non-Avana H47; and driver refinements H43 adenocarcinoma KRAS-wildtype EGFR amplification or H45 KRAS mutation excluding BRAF V600E. Technical POLR2A library benchmarks H34 and H58 can be deferred unless screen-quality artifacts emerge. If a borderline driver effect becomes both precise and clinically meaningful, I would request one targeted validation then; no validation request is needed now because the newly validated H15 and H8 evidence have been incorporated.\n\nOverall conclusion: the strongest defensible claims are internally supported dataset associations, not causal or generalizable therapeutic statements. The supplied dataset supports SMARCA4-loss/SMARCA2, MSI-high/WRN, KEAP1-loss/weaker-NFE2L2, and TP53-loss/weaker-USP7 associations, while rejecting several canonical or mechanistically plausible comparisons and leaving RTK/driver and some library/batch context questions unresolved.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 3 synthesis update.\n\nAccepted dataset associations after new validation: 1 SMARCA4 loss -> stronger SMARCA2; full H7/R7, ARID1A-intact H15/R15+R39 validated, batch_a H25/R16, non-Avana H38/R27, TP53-intact H52/R34. 2 MSI-high -> stronger WRN; H9/R9, Avana H26/R17, BRCA2-intact H18/R18, TP53-intact H53/R35. 3 KEAP1 loss -> weaker NFE2L2; original H8 rejected and closed with R8+R13, reverse H14 accepted/validated, non-Avana H46/R29, TP53-intact H54/R36. 4 TP53 loss -> weaker USP7; H20 validated R23+R26, non-Avana H41/R28, SMARCA4-intact H51/R33.\n\nClosed rejects: H2 KRAS STAT3; H5 BRAF BRAF KRAS-WT; H8 KEAP1 stronger NFE2L2; H10 MYC DHX9; H11 MYC RPL11; H13 ARID1A SMARCA2 overall; H27 ERBB2 amp ERBB2 KRAS-WT; H32 KRAS RIT1; H59 marker D control.\n\nUnresolved: H1 KRAS KRAS; H3/H24/H28 EGFR contexts; H33 KIF18A TP53; H4 overall ERBB2; H6 TP53 MDM2; H12 POLR2A Avana; H17 BRCA2 WRN; H55 MYC MDM2 TP53-WT; H48/H49/H50 library interactions.\n\nNext analysis priorities: H16, H39, H21, H31 SMARCA2; H35, H36, H42, H37 USP7; H22 KEAP1 STK11; H40 WRN batch_b; H47 KIF18A non-Avana; H43 or H45 driver; H34/H58 later. No validation now; request only if borderline driver becomes precise and clinically meaningful. All claims remain dataset-specific associations, not causal or generalizable."
}

