  "narrative": "I assessed the twelve newly delivered comparisons using the direction-specific signed estimates and approximate outcome-specific 10%-of-absolute-mean clinical scales: SMARCA2 about 0.042, WRN about 0.030, NFE2L2 and USP7 about 0.009, KIF18A about 0.026, and EGFR or KRAS about 0.024 to 0.025. For direction -1, a positive signed estimate means stronger dependency in the exposed group; for direction +1, a positive signed estimate means weaker dependency in the exposed group. H82 through H85 test whether the four previously supported dataset-specific signals persist when lower-Cas9 screens are excluded using cas9_activity_score at least 0.846. All eight estimates remain enormous and direction-consistent: SMARCA4 loss is associated with stronger SMARCA2 dependency at +0.7909 with interval +0.7657 to +0.8161; MSI-high is associated with stronger WRN dependency at +0.7938 with interval +0.7567 to +0.8310; KEAP1 loss is associated with weaker NFE2L2 dependency at +0.7980 with interval +0.7732 to +0.8228; and TP53 loss is associated with weaker USP7 dependency at +0.8109 with interval +0.7865 to +0.8353. The 95% Welch lower bounds are far above both null and clinical irrelevance on their outcome scales. I accept H82, H83, H84, and H85 as dataset-specific robustness associations and close each exact context test. These are internal strata overlapping the original populations, not independent replication, and the Cas9 restriction is a selection on measured screen quality; nevertheless, attenuation would have been the warning signal, and none occurred. H86 through H89 test the same four signals within lung adenocarcinoma, the dominant subtype. Estimates are similarly large: SMARCA4 loss plus SMARCA2 +0.7770 with interval +0.7554 to +0.7987; MSI-high plus WRN +0.7838 with interval +0.7479 to +0.8196; KEAP1 loss plus NFE2L2 +0.7929 with interval +0.7692 to +0.8167; and TP53 loss plus USP7 +0.7990 with interval +0.7755 to +0.8225. I accept H86 through H89 and close the exact adenocarcinoma context tests. Again, these are internal consistency tests and do not imply causality or external generalizability. H90 tests whether the KRAS-mutation association with KRAS dependency differs between adenocarcinoma and non-adenocarcinoma. The signed interaction is near null at -0.0065 with interval -0.0447 to +0.0317. The interval includes clinically meaningful positive, null, and negative values, so it neither supports subtype-specific KRAS dependency modification nor decisively rejects it. I leave H90 unresolved and deferred. H91 is the most informative new mechanistic result: NF1 loss within KRAS-wildtype cells is associated with weaker KRAS dependency, signed +0.0387 with interval +0.0062 to +0.0712. The point estimate is approximately 15.8% of the absolute mean KRAS dependency score and the 95% interval excludes null, but the lower bound is below the approximate 10% clinical threshold of 0.0245. Therefore the discovery evidence supports a likely positive association in the KRAS-wildtype context, but it is not yet confident enough to declare a clinically meaningful effect in this supplied dataset. I leave H91 unresolved and active. Critical alternatives remain: NF1 loss may co-occur with other RAS or MAPK-pathway alterations, selection among KRAS-wildtype lines may be uneven, and the effect may be attenuated by screen-quality or driver-background confounding. I register two refinements to test screen-quality robustness and BRAF-background specificity before considering validation. H92 tests BRAF V600E within KRAS-wildtype cells for weaker KRAS dependency, signed +0.0149 with interval -0.0160 to +0.0459. It is directionally in the bypass direction but imprecise and crosses null, so it does not support or exclude a clinically meaningful effect. I leave H92 unresolved and deferred. H93 refines the PTEN-loss EGFR-dependency question in KRAS-wildtype adenocarcinoma. For the registered weaker-EGFR-dependency direction, the signed estimate is -0.0305 with interval -0.0633 to +0.0023. The upper bound is below the approximate EGFR clinical threshold of 0.025, so this dataset rejects the claim that PTEN loss is associated with clinically meaningful weaker EGFR dependency in this context. The negative estimate also raises the possibility of the opposite relationship, i.e. stronger EGFR dependency, but that is a new hypothesis rather than confirmation of H93. I close H93 as rejected and register a separate opposite-direction refinement. I do not request validation now. The accepted context tests for the four large signals are robust, internally replicated, and partially directly validated already; additional validation would be redundant. H91 is intriguing but not yet threshold-supported by its lower bound, and validation intervals are adjusted for the maximum number of validation comparisons; it is more informative first to assess high-Cas9 and BRAF-background robustness or to wait for a sharper driver result. Overall, the defensible conclusion is that the supplied dataset provides highly robust associations for SMARCA4 loss with stronger SMARCA2 dependency, MSI-high with stronger WRN dependency, KEAP1 loss with weaker NFE2L2 dependency, and TP53 loss with weaker USP7 dependency across high-Cas9 and adenocarcinoma strata, while the NF1-loss bypass hypothesis remains a promising unresolved dataset-specific signal and PTEN-loss weaker EGFR dependency is rejected in KRAS-wildtype adenocarcinoma.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 5 appraise update. The four primary accepted dataset-specific associations are now internally robust in both high-Cas9 screens and lung adenocarcinoma: SMARCA4 loss and stronger SMARCA2 dependency; MSI-high and stronger WRN dependency; KEAP1 loss and weaker NFE2L2 dependency; TP53 loss and weaker USP7 dependency. These are associations within the supplied dataset, not causal claims. H91 is the key unresolved new driver hypothesis: NF1 loss in KRAS-wildtype cells shows signed +0.0387 weaker KRAS dependency with CI +0.0062 to +0.0712; it excludes null but lower bound is below the 10% threshold, so keep active and avoid validation until or unless screen-quality and BRAF-background refinements remain large. H90 and H92 are unresolved driver/subtype tests. H93 rejects PTEN-loss weaker EGFR dependency in KRAS-wildtype adenocarcinoma; register reverse-direction PTEN stronger EGFR dependency as a new hypothesis but do not treat it as accepted. Next analyses should test H91 refinements and remaining context/driver completions; validation should be reserved for a driver or specificity signal that is both precise and clinically meaningful, or if a newly supported context result is genuinely novel and actionable."
