  "narrative": "This synthesis resolves the H15 response deadline using the independent validation result R39. H15 tests SMARCA4 loss versus SMARCA2 dependency among ARID1A-intact samples with direction -1; because the result is already signed to the hypothesis direction, a positive estimate means that SMARCA4-loss samples have a more negative dependency score, i.e. stronger SMARCA2 dependency. Discovery R15 estimated +0.7770 with 95% Welch interval +0.7558 to +0.7983 in ARID1A-intact samples. Independent validation R39 estimated +0.7846 with interval +0.7517 to +0.8174; this interval accounts for the maximum number of validation comparisons in the run. The approximate 10%-of-absolute-mean clinical threshold for dependency_SMARCA2 is about 0.042, so both the discovery and the adjusted independent validation interval are far above both null and clinical irrelevance. The validation estimate is essentially unchanged from discovery. I therefore accept H15 and close the exact ARID1A-intact SMARCA4-loss versus SMARCA2-dependency comparison as supported in the supplied dataset. This remains a dataset-specific association, not proof that SMARCA4 loss causally causes SMARCA2 dependency or that the effect will generalize to other cohorts or clinical settings.\n\nThe current supported scientific findings in this supplied lung-cell-line dataset are concentrated in four very large dependency associations. First, SMARCA4 loss is associated with stronger SMARCA2 dependency. The overall comparison is accepted at +0.7840, and the exact ARID1A-intact context is now independently validated through H15/R15/R39. The same association remains very large across multiple internal strata, including batch_a, non-Avana, TP53-intact, KEAP1-intact, KRAS-wildtype, high-Cas9, and lung adenocarcinoma samples. This gives a highly stable supplied-dataset signal, although it does not establish synthetic lethality causality or clinical applicability. Second, MSI-high is associated with stronger WRN dependency. The overall comparison is accepted at +0.7899, and the signal is large in Avana, BRCA2-intact, TP53-intact, KEAP1-intact, STK11-intact, high-Cas9, and adenocarcinoma contexts. The exact overall comparison has not been independently validated, but the internal context replication is extremely consistent and every tested stratum far exceeds the approximate WRN threshold of 0.030. Third, KEAP1 loss is associated with weaker NFE2L2 dependency, opposite the originally registered direction. H8 remains rejected and closed because under its original stronger-NFE2L2-dependency direction the evidence is strongly negative, including validation R13. The reversed claim H14 is accepted with discovery +0.7961 and independent validation +0.8096, far above the approximate NFE2L2 threshold of 0.009, and the association remains very large in non-Avana, TP53-intact, STK11-intact, KRAS-wildtype, high-Cas9, and adenocarcinoma contexts. Fourth, TP53 loss is associated with weaker USP7 dependency. H20 is accepted and independently validated through R26, and the effect remains very large in non-Avana, SMARCA4-intact, KEAP1-intact, high-Cas9, and adenocarcinoma contexts. The exact overall TP53-loss versus USP7-dependency question is now direct-validated; broader context tests remain descriptive and not necessary for establishing the main association. Across all four headline signals, the interpretation should be association within this supplied dataset, not causal inference and not external generalizability.\n\nThe dataset also provides informative negative or rejected findings for several canonical or constructed predictors. It rejects KRAS-mutation-associated stronger STAT3 dependency H2, BRAF V600E-associated stronger BRAF dependency among KRAS-wildtype samples H5, the original KEAP1-loss stronger-NFE2L2 hypothesis H8, MYC amplification-associated stronger DHX9 H10, MYC amplification-associated stronger RPL11 H11, ARID1A loss-associated stronger SMARCA2 overall H13, ERBB2 amplification-associated stronger ERBB2 among KRAS-wildtype samples H27, KRAS-mutation-associated weaker RIT1 H32, and research marker D as a clinically meaningful SMARCA2 predictor H59. PTEN loss was not supported as a driver of weaker EGFR dependency either overall H73 or in KRAS-wildtype adenocarcinoma H93. These rejections are limited to the tested comparisons and do not imply universal biological absence.\n\nUnresolved uncertainty remains mainly in the driver, bypass, and technical-context programs. The overall KRAS-mutation effect on KRAS dependency is directionally consistent with stronger dependency but imprecise and below robust acceptance: +0.0137, interval -0.0038 to +0.0312. EGFR amplification overall is borderline: +0.0252, interval -0.0003 to +0.0507, and subgroup contexts remain unresolved. TP53 loss associated with stronger KIF18A dependency narrowly excludes null at +0.0208, interval +0.0012 to +0.0403, but its point estimate and lower bound are below the approximate KIF18A threshold, so it remains hypothesis-generating rather than accepted. NF1 loss within KRAS-wildtype cells is the most informative unresolved mechanistic signal: +0.0387, interval +0.0062 to +0.0712. This excludes null, but the lower bound is below the approximate KRAS clinical threshold of 0.0245, so I keep H91 unresolved and active. The reverse-direction PTEN-loss stronger-EGFR-dependency claim H96 is similarly promising but not established: +0.0305, interval -0.0023 to +0.0633. Library interaction and batch tests for the four large signals are not decisive and do not appear to threaten the main conclusions: SMARCA2/SMARCA4 library interaction is imprecise, NFE2L2/KEAP1 library interaction is near null, and USP7/TP53 library interaction is wide. Matched RNA/DNA omics completeness is near null for SMARCA2, although its upper bound slightly exceeds threshold, so this remains a weak technical concern rather than evidence of artifact. Other unresolved items include pending EGFR, ERBB2, KRAS, PIK3CA, KIF18A, POLR2A, TMED10, research-marker, and matched-omics context tests.\n\nThe most informative next analyses are mechanistic refinements rather than additional validation of the already accepted large signals. Priority should be given to the registered NF1-loss refinements H94 and H95, which test the KRAS-wildtype weaker-KRAS-dependency signal under high Cas9 activity and after excluding BRAF V600E background. If one or both remain above the KRAS threshold with lower bounds also above threshold, NF1-loss in KRAS-wildtype cells would become a strong candidate for targeted independent validation. If they are attenuated, the H91 signal would be less compelling and likely context dependent. Secondary priority should be completing the already registered driver and context grid for unresolved canonical predictors, especially EGFR amplification in adenocarcinoma KRAS-wildtype cells, ERBB2 amplification in KRAS-wildtype contexts, KRAS-mutation effects stratified by subtype, BRAF background, STK11 status, and TP53 interaction, and PIK3CA/PTEN/ERBB2 bypass hypotheses. The accepted-signal context grid should be treated as descriptive completeness work: pending library, batch, subtype, and interaction tests may refine interpretation but are not necessary for the main conclusions. I would reserve validation budget for a new driver or specificity effect that is both precise and clinically meaningful, rather than for repeated validation of SMARCA4/SMARCA2, MSI/WRN, KEAP1/NFE2L2, or TP53/USP7.\n\nOverall, the defensible report at this point is that the supplied dataset strongly and internally replicably associates four measured predictors with clinically large CRISPR dependency differences: SMARCA4 loss with stronger SMARCA2 dependency, MSI-high with stronger WRN dependency, KEAP1 loss with weaker NFE2L2 dependency, and TP53 loss with weaker USP7 dependency. The original KEAP1-loss stronger-NFE2L2 hypothesis is rejected, and several canonical driver and RTK bypass hypotheses remain negative, imprecise, or unresolved. No result should be stated as causal, clinically actionable, or generalizable beyond this dataset without external replication and experimental validation.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 5 synthesis update. H15 independently validated and closed: SMARCA4 loss-associated stronger SMARCA2 dependency in ARID1A-intact cells is robust (discovery +0.7770; validation +0.7846; adjusted interval +0.7517 to +0.8174; threshold about 0.042). Four headline dataset associations remain accepted: SMARCA4 loss/SMARCA2, MSI-high/WRN, KEAP1 loss/weaker NFE2L2 (reversed H14 accepted, original H8 rejected), and TP53 loss/weaker USP7. Interpretation remains dataset-specific association, not causality or generalizability. Next priority: analyze H94 and H95 NF1-loss KRAS-wildtype refinements. If robust and above KRAS threshold about 0.0245, H91 becomes a validation candidate. Otherwise leave unresolved/downweight. Secondary: complete driver/context grid for EGFR, ERBB2, KRAS, PIK3CA, KIF18A, POLR2A, TMED10, matched omics, and accepted-signal library/batch/subtype interactions. Reserve validation budget for a newly sharp driver or specificity result, not repeated validation of main accepted signals."
