  "narrative": "I assessed the newly released validation evidence using the signed convention and approximate outcome-specific 10%-of-absolute-mean clinical scales: KRAS about 0.0245, EGFR about 0.025, DHX9 about 0.025. For direction -1, a positive signed estimate means stronger dependency in the exposed group; for direction +1, a positive signed estimate means weaker dependency. H95 tested whether NF1 loss is associated with weaker KRAS dependency among KRAS-wildtype, BRAF-wildtype cells. Discovery R65 supported that direction at +0.0360 with 95% Welch interval +0.0033 to +0.0688, excluding null but with the lower bound below the clinical scale. The independent adjusted validation R76 was not consistent: signed estimate -0.0161 with interval -0.0621 to +0.0299. Its point estimate is in the opposite direction, crosses null, and only barely reaches the clinical scale in the upper tail. Therefore the exact NF1-loss, KRAS/BRAF-wildtype context is not supported as a clinically meaningful dataset association, and I reject H95 as a scientific conclusion. This is not evidence that NF1 loss universally has no relationship to KRAS dependency; rather, the cleanest biologically motivated test did not replicate the initially promising NF1-loss bypass signal. Consequently, I downgrade the broader NF1-loss program: H91, H94, H97, H98, and overall NF1-loss H75 remain unresolved but are deferred rather than active priorities. H10 tested MYC amplification-associated stronger DHX9 dependency. Discovery R10 was near null at -0.0028 with interval -0.0244 to +0.0189. Independent validation R77 was signed -0.0264 with adjusted interval -0.0595 to +0.0066; the point is opposite the hypothesized stronger-dependency direction and the upper bound is below the approximate DHX9 clinical threshold. I therefore reject H10 with additional independent support. The main accepted supplied-dataset signals remain unchanged: SMARCA4 loss with stronger SMARCA2 dependency, MSI-high with stronger WRN dependency, KEAP1 loss with weaker NFE2L2 dependency, and TP53 loss with weaker USP7 dependency. H15 was previously accepted and closed after independent validation. H10 adds an independent validation-backed rejection for MYC amplification and DHX9 dependency. The next most informative program is PTEN-loss associated with stronger EGFR dependency. H105 showed +0.0435 with interval +0.0047 to +0.0824 only in high-Cas9 KRAS-wildtype adenocarcinoma, while H96 was borderline in the same subtype without Cas9 restriction. Before validating H105, I registered specificity tests across lower Cas9 activity, PIK3CA-wildtype status, KRAS-mutant background, KRAS dependency, and POLR2A dependency. These will test whether the EGFR signal is robust, pathway-specific, and not a context or technical artifact. All conclusions remain associations within the supplied dataset, not causal claims or claims of clinical generalizability.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 6 synthesize update. Validation results: H95 NF1-loss weaker KRAS dependency in KRAS/BRAF-wildtype cells failed independent validation and is rejected closed; this deprioritizes the NF1-loss bypass signal. H10 MYC amplification stronger DHX9 dependency is rejected with independent validation R77. Accepted headline supplied-dataset associations remain: SMARCA4 loss stronger SMARCA2 dependency; MSI-high stronger WRN dependency; KEAP1 loss weaker NFE2L2 dependency; TP53 loss weaker USP7 dependency. Exact ARID1A-intact SMARCA4/SMARCA2 and overall TP53/USP7 have direct validation. NF1-related claims H91, H94, H97, H98, H75 remain unresolved but deferred because validation of the cleanest context did not support clinically meaningful replication. The next active promising signal is PTEN-loss stronger EGFR dependency: H105 is high-Cas9 KRAS-wildtype adenocarcinoma specific with signed +0.0435, interval +0.0047 to +0.0824; H96 is the same subtype without Cas9 restriction at +0.0305, interval -0.0023 to +0.0633. Registered new specificity tests: low-Cas9 PTEN-loss EGFR; high-Cas9 KRAS-wt adeno PIK3CA-wt PTEN-loss EGFR; PTEN-loss KRAS dependency as pathway-specific control; PTEN-loss POLR2A as global specificity control; PTEN-loss EGFR in KRAS-mutant high-Cas9 background. Do not validate H105 until these specificity tests support robustness. Interpret all findings as supplied-dataset associations, not causality, clinical actionability, or external generalizability."
