  "narrative": "Signed convention used throughout: for direction -1, a positive signed estimate means stronger, more negative dependency in the exposed group; for direction +1, a positive signed estimate means weaker, less negative dependency in the exposed group. For EGFR dependency, the approximate absolute-mean clinical scale is about 0.025.\n\nH105 is the PTEN-loss versus EGFR-wildtype KRAS-wildtype adenocarcinoma high-Cas9 stronger-EGFR-dependency claim. Discovery R74 was the best selected context: signed +0.0435 with interval +0.0047 to +0.0824. It excluded the null but not the clinical scale, and it had emerged through several post hoc contextual filters. The independent validation R90 did not replicate the selected signal: signed estimate +0.0148, adjusted interval -0.0444 to +0.0740, cells 87 exposed and 528 comparator. The point estimate is below the EGFR clinical scale, crosses null at the adjusted validation level, and the interval still contains both clinically meaningful positive values and null or negative values. Taken together, this is not evidence supporting a clinically meaningful association in the exact H105 context; it is better interpreted as a discovery selected context that failed independent replication. I therefore reject H105 and close that exact claim.\n\nThis failure downgrades the PTEN-loss EGFR-dependency program. The exact claim was the most precise and most favorable of the overlapping PTEN contexts, so the broader discovery-only contexts H96, H108, and H112 are now lower priority and I defer them while leaving their status unresolved rather than accepting them. The related null interaction H114 remains deferred: it did not show clear Cas9-activity enrichment, but it was imprecise. The low-Cas9 context H107 remains deferred because uncertainty is too large. H111, PTEN loss in KRAS-mutant high-Cas9 screens, remains an active specificity control because if a real PTEN-loss EGFR effect exists, it should not show a comparable positive effect in KRAS-mutant cells; if it does, the signal is less EGFR-specific. However, further validation spending on PTEN is not justified now.\n\nThe PTEN specificity controls also remain informative for interpretation but not decisive. H109 rejected PTEN-associated weaker KRAS dependency in the clean high-Cas9 KRAS-wildtype adenocarcinoma context: R83 was signed -0.0127 with interval -0.0498 to +0.0245, opposite the weaker-KRAS claim and below the clinically meaningful upper tail for that registered direction. H113 did not support PTEN-associated stronger ERBB2 dependency, R82 signed -0.0043 interval -0.0454 to +0.0367, but it cannot exclude a clinically meaningful positive ERBB2 effect, so ERBB2-specificity remains unresolved. H110 tested PTEN-associated stronger POLR2A dependency, R84 signed +0.0024 interval -0.0394 to +0.0442, near null but not conclusive because the interval reaches the clinical scale. Thus the evidence does not show a broad PTEN-associated global dependency shift, but it also does not provide clean positive evidence for an EGFR-specific PTEN effect.\n\nThe overall dataset-specific scientific picture now separates into robust accepted associations, robust negative or validation-failed signals, and unresolved borderline signals. Accepted supplied-dataset associations remain dominated by very large effects: SMARCA4 loss is associated with much stronger SMARCA2 dependency, including validated ARID1A-wildtype and many context refinements; MSI-high is associated with much stronger WRN dependency; KEAP1 loss is associated with much weaker NFE2L2 dependency, with the original stronger-NFE2L2 direction rejected; and TP53 loss is associated with much weaker USP7 dependency, validated independently. These associations are not causal or generalizable claims; they are dataset-specific patterns.\n\nNegative or validation-failed signals now include H95, where NF1-loss associated weaker KRAS dependency in KRAS/BRAF-wildtype cells failed independent validation, leading to rejection and downgrading of the NF1 bypass program; H10, where MYC amplification associated stronger DHX9 dependency failed independent validation; H105, PTEN-loss associated stronger EGFR dependency in high-Cas9 KRAS-wildtype adenocarcinoma; H109, PTEN-loss associated weaker KRAS dependency; H122 and H123, which reject broad POLR2A shifts for SMARCA4 loss and KEAP1 loss and therefore support some outcome specificity of those large signals; and other exact context rejections such as ERBB2 amplification in the KRAS-wildtype adenocarcinoma high-Cas9 context H102.\n\nUnresolved uncertainty remains around several smaller signals. EGFR amplification-associated stronger EGFR dependency in H3, H24, and H28 is near threshold in some contexts but not supported with acceptable uncertainty. KRAS mutation-associated stronger KRAS dependency remains weakly imprecise. KIF18A TP53-loss signals H33, H115, and H116 are borderline or near null, so the KIF18A program is not established. POLR2A TP53-loss H124 remains unresolved because its interval includes clinically meaningful values, motivating the new high-Cas9 POLR2A specificity test. The accepted-signal robustness proposals for high-Cas9 adenocarcinoma are next useful analyses to test whether the large accepted associations persist simultaneously in the most favorable subtype and screening-quality context, while existing untested context-completeness claims H117-H121, STAT3 SMAD4 H125, PTEN KRAS-mutant specificity H111, and marker controls H78/H79 remain useful for negative-control and boundary testing. Overall, the most scientifically meaningful supported findings are still the four large dependency-specific associations; the new PTEN EGFR validation does not add a new supported finding and redirects effort toward coherence and specificity rather than further PTEN pursuit.",
