  "narrative": "I used the signed convention already in force: for direction -1, a positive estimate means the exposed group has a stronger, more negative dependency score than the comparator; for direction +1, a positive estimate means the exposed group has a weaker, less negative dependency score. Clinical importance was judged against a roughly 10%-of-absolute-mean scale for each outcome: SMARCA2 approximately 0.0415, WRN approximately 0.0301, NFE2L2 approximately 0.0093, USP7 approximately 0.0093, DHX9 approximately 0.0249, MDM2 approximately 0.0269, BRAF approximately 0.0248, EGFR approximately 0.025, and KRAS approximately 0.0245. These are supplied-dataset discovery associations, not causal or externally generalizable claims.\n\nThe high-Cas9 adenocarcinoma context tests for the four accepted large signals were uniformly strong. H127, SMARCA2 dependency in SMARCA4-loss adenocarcinoma screens with Cas9 activity at least 0.846, gave R91 = +0.7852, interval +0.7575 to +0.8129. H128, WRN dependency in MSI-high adenocarcinoma high-Cas9 screens, gave R92 = +0.7831, interval +0.7404 to +0.8257. H129, weaker NFE2L2 dependency in KEAP1-loss adenocarcinoma high-Cas9 screens, gave R93 = +0.7859, interval +0.7559 to +0.8158. H130, weaker USP7 dependency in TP53-loss adenocarcinoma high-Cas9 screens, gave R94 = +0.8136, interval +0.7840 to +0.8431. For each outcome, the lower bound is many times the relevant clinical scale, so the exact high-quality subtype contexts are clinically supported with very favorable uncertainty. I accepted and closed these exact context claims because their scientific question, whether the headline signals persist under both adenocarcinoma and high-Cas9 screening restrictions, is now resolved in the affirmative. This strengthens the internal coherence of the accepted large associations but does not imply causality or generalizability beyond the dataset.\n\nThe ARID1A co-loss context produced a nuanced but consistent result. H132 tests SMARCA4 loss in lines that also have ARID1A loss: R96 = +0.8143, interval +0.7666 to +0.8620. This is again far above the SMARCA2 clinical threshold, so the SMARCA4-loss associated stronger SMARCA2 dependency remains extremely large when ARID1A is also lost. I accepted and closed H132. By contrast, H21 tests whether the SMARCA4-loss effect formally differs by ARID1A status: R95 = +0.0373, interval -0.0148 to +0.0894. The point estimate is modest relative to SMARCA2 clinical scale, and the interval crosses null while also reaching above threshold. This neither establishes an important positive interaction nor cleanly excludes a moderate interaction. H21 remains unresolved and deferred; H132 is more scientifically informative as an exact context effect, whereas the interaction remains imprecise.\n\nThe specificity and secondary-pathway controls mostly produced clinically meaningful nulls or opposite effects. H135 tests whether SMARCA4 loss also produces stronger DHX9 dependency. R97 = -0.0055, interval -0.0249 to +0.0138. For direction -1, the clinically meaningful claim requires positive effects above about +0.0249; the upper bound is below that, so the DHX9 specificity control rejects a clinically meaningful stronger DHX9 effect. This supports the outcome specificity of the large SMARCA4/SMARCA2 association. H136 tests whether TP53 loss is associated with stronger WRN dependency. R98 = -0.0054, interval -0.0342 to +0.0235. The upper bound is below the WRN clinical scale of about +0.0301, so the registered stronger-WRN TP53-loss claim is rejected. H142 tests whether KRAS mutation is associated with weaker BRAF dependency, R100 = -0.0164, interval -0.0343 to +0.0014. The interval is centered on the opposite of the weaker-BRAF hypothesis and the upper bound is far below the BRAF clinical scale, so the claim is rejected. H111 tests whether PTEN loss is associated with stronger EGFR dependency among KRAS-mutant high-Cas9 screens. R101 = -0.0351, interval -0.0812 to +0.0111. The positive upper bound is below the EGFR clinical scale, and the point estimate is in the opposite direction, so the registered stronger-EGFR claim is rejected. This helps the already downgraded PTEN-loss EGFR program: the signal does not appear to extend into KRAS-mutant high-Cas9 cells, although the PTEN program as a whole is not currently supported.\n\nTwo smaller programs remain scientifically possible but not established. H138 refines the MDM2 TP53-loss test in high-Cas9 screens: R99 = +0.0226, interval -0.0099 to +0.0552. For direction +1, this means weaker MDM2 dependency with TP53 loss, which is biologically plausible through the canonical p53-MDM2 axis. The point estimate is below the MDM2 clinical scale of about +0.0269, but the upper bound exceeds it. The exact claim is unresolved and deferred rather than rejected because the interval does not exclude a clinically meaningful effect. H23 tests KRAS mutation versus wild-type KRAS dependency in adenocarcinoma: R102 = +0.0115, interval -0.0094 to +0.0324. The point is below the KRAS clinical scale but the upper bound exceeds it, so the exact claim remains unresolved and active. It is a low-confidence candidate, and the pending high-Cas9 adenocarcinoma refinement H139 is more informative than pursuing this broad claim further now.\n\nFor validation, I request H9, the original MSI-high versus MSI-non-high WRN dependency claim. It has one of the four headline large effects, R9 = +0.7899, but unlike several other headline claims it has not been independently validated. The new H128 result strengthens its coherence in a clean high-Cas9 adenocarcinoma context, so independent validation of the core MSI-high WRN association is now useful. If validation is favorable, the WRN MSI-high association becomes much more robust; if validation is unfavorable, WRN/MSI-high should be downgraded alongside the accepted large-signal set. No further validation is requested for PTEN or EGFR at this point because the exact best-selected PTEN EGFR context already failed independent validation.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 8 appraise update. Signed convention retained. The four accepted large dependency associations now have clinically supported exact high-Cas9 adenocarcinoma contexts: SMARCA4 loss with much stronger SMARCA2 dependency, H127; MSI-high with much stronger WRN dependency, H128; KEAP1 loss with much weaker NFE2L2 dependency, H129; and TP53 loss with much weaker USP7 dependency, H130. All exact context lower bounds are far above their outcome-specific clinical scales. These remain dataset-specific associations, not causal or generalizable claims. ARID1A co-loss context H132 remains robust for SMARCA2, but the formal interaction H21 is imprecise and deferred.\n\nSpecificity controls strengthened the accepted programs: SMARCA4 loss is not associated with clinically meaningful stronger DHX9 dependency, H135 rejected; TP53 loss is not associated with clinically meaningful stronger WRN dependency, H136 rejected; KRAS mutation is not associated with weaker BRAF dependency, H142 rejected; PTEN loss is not associated with stronger EGFR dependency in KRAS-mutant high-Cas9 screens, H111 rejected. The PTEN EGFR program remains downgraded after H105 validation failure, and these specificity results argue against a broad KRAS-mutant extension of that program.\n\nRemaining unresolved borderline signals: MDM2 TP53-loss high-Cas9 H138, R99 = +0.0226, interval -0.0099 to +0.0552, biologically plausible but imprecise; KRAS mutation stronger KRAS dependency in adenocarcinoma H23, R102 = +0.0115, interval -0.0094 to +0.0324, low confidence. Pending next analyses should prioritize specificity/completeness: H126 and H133/H134 POLR2A TP53/SMARCA4/KEAP1 controls, H118/H119/H120/H121 accepted-signal context completeness, H125 STAT3 SMAD4, H137 RPL11 TP53, H139 KRAS high-Cas9 adenocarcinoma, and if slots permit EGFR/ERBB2 specificity controls H140/H141. Requested validation: H9 MSI-high WRN dependency, core large effect not yet independently validated.",
